1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,700
Moving on to gallbladder and biliary tract, the first disorder is a congenital disorder.

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,560
This is called biliary atresia.

3
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:11,540
In biliary atresia, remember the word atretic means that you fail to form the lumen of a tube.

4
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:16,820
And so in this particular case, you fail to form the lumen of the extrahepatic biliary tree.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:19,960
So it's the bile ducts that are outside of the liver.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,720
This, of course, leads to biliary obstruction within the first three months of life.

7
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:29,140
Now, there's a range here because sometimes the patients can have a congenital failure to form this tree,

8
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:34,460
or sometimes it can be that they form the tree, but that it actually gets destroyed, for example, by infection.

9
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:38,800
Regardless, they generally present with neonatal jaundice very early in life.

10
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:41,900
This jaundice would be due to conjugated bilirubin.

11
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:45,000
That's because, of course, in the bile is conjugated bilirubin,

12
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:47,260
and so that conjugated bilirubin would leak into the blood.

13
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,320
Eventually, because there's going to be back pressure into the liver,

14
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:55,160
that's going to result in damage to the liver cells, and this can eventually progress to cirrhosis.

15
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,140
Chololithiasis refers to gallbladder stones,

16
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:02,780
and it's basically due to precipitation of cholesterol or bilirubin in the bile.

17
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:06,960
Now, the idea here is that you get either a high concentration of cholesterol

18
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:09,620
or a high concentration of bilirubin in the bile.

19
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:12,900
Remember, both cholesterol and bilirubin are components of bile,

20
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:17,820
and this high concentration results in a precipitation of either of these two components.

21
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:21,780
So the two types of stones, actually, are cholesterol stones and bilirubin stones.

22
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:25,820
Now, this would occur due to supersaturation, and that's, again, I've already discussed that,

23
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:27,900
a high concentration of one of these two components.

24
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:31,880
In addition, this could occur when there's decreased phospholipids or bile acids.

25
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:37,240
Now, recall that phospholipids and bile acids, they help to solubilize the cholesterol, for example,

26
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:40,480
and so if you lose the ability to solubilize the cholesterol,

27
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:43,620
you're going to increase the risk that cholesterol would precipitate.

28
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:50,260
One example of this would be a farm tie-in in which you think about a drug, for example, cholestyramine.

29
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:54,480
Cholestyramine is a lipid-lowering agent, and the way it works is by binding bile acids,

30
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,600
which then prohibits recycling of the bile acids by the ileum.

31
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:01,560
Recall that the ileum recycles bile acids and brings it back into the blood.

32
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:06,420
And so, for example, if you had cholestyramine, which would bind the bile acids and prohibit recycling,

33
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:12,340
then that would decrease the bile acids, and, of course, a decreased bile acid would increase the risk of a cholesterol stone.

34
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:17,840
And that's an important side effect of cholestyramine, which is that the patients can get cholesterol stones,

35
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:20,680
and that's particularly high yield, so I took a little diversion there.

36
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,920
In addition, stasis can increase the risk of formation of a stone,

37
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:28,320
and one example of this would be that when you have stasis, you have increased risk of growth of bacteria.

38
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:31,240
Bacteria can deconjugate, for example, the bilirubin,

39
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:36,920
and it's this type of deconjugated bilirubin in the bile that actually leads to the formation of bilirubin gallstones.

40
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:41,060
Cholesterol stones are the most common type of stone, especially in the West,

41
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:45,020
and they're usually radiolucent, so they don't show up on x-ray.

42
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:48,660
They can, on rare occasions, show up on x-ray, especially if there's associated calcium,

43
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,720
but generally speaking, they're radiolucent.

44
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:54,860
Here's a picture of cholesterol stones, and the key thing here, you can see that this is the gallbladder,

45
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:57,780
and the most important thing is that you see these yellow stones,

46
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,900
and that's the characteristic of cholesterol stones, that they have a yellow color.

47
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:04,980
The risk factors include age, and the average age is around 40.

48
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:10,140
Estrogen is another risk factor, and so female gender would also be an important risk factor.

49
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:15,700
Now, the reason this is a risk factor is because estrogens increase the activity of HMG-CoA reductase.

50
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:20,840
Of course, increased activity of HMG-CoA reductase would result in increased cholesterol synthesis.

51
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:25,900
Estrogens can also increase the lipoprotein receptors on the hepatocytes,

52
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,500
which would then increase cholesterol uptake,

53
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:33,020
and so these are the two ways by which estrogen, and hence female gender,

54
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:36,180
increases the risk for the formation of cholesterol stones.

55
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:39,380
Clofibrate can also increase the risk. This is a drug.

56
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:44,060
It's a lipid-lowering agent that increases HMG-CoA reductase activity,

57
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:47,380
hence increasing the amount of cholesterol produced,

58
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:50,740
and also decreases conversion of cholesterol to bile acids,

59
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:54,880
and so if you don't have enough bile acids, the cholesterol will be less soluble within the fluid,

60
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,840
and hence it could potentially precipitate.

61
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:00,420
There's an increased incidence in certain genetic subgroups.

62
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:03,160
For example, Native American ethnicity increases the risk.

63
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,020
Crohn's disease increases the risk,

64
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:09,900
and you're well aware of the fact that the most common location for Crohn's disease is the terminal ileum,

65
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:14,320
so when you damage the ileum, that decreases the reuptake of bile salts and bile acids,

66
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:17,460
and of course, if you don't have good reuptake of bile salts and bile acids,

67
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:23,880
those bile salts and acids are necessary for allowing cholesterol to solubilize within the bile,

68
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:26,160
and so therefore, if you have less solubilization,

69
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:28,620
you're going to have an increased risk for precipitation of cholesterol.

70
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:31,660
And finally, cirrhosis would increase the risk,

71
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:35,800
and that's because when there's cirrhosis, there's decreased production of bile salts by the hepatocytes.

72
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:38,540
So again, cholesterol would not be able to solubilize,

73
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:41,040
and hence, there would be an increased risk of precipitation.

74
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:44,860
Bilirubin stones are usually radio-opaque, so they show up on x-ray,

75
00:04:45,180 --> 00:04:48,200
and the classic feature of bilirubin stone is that it would black in color.

76
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:52,320
Now, the risk factors for bilirubin stones are basically extravascular hemolysis.

77
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:58,240
Again, in extravascular hemolysis, you get a large amount of production of unconjugated bilirubin

78
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:02,340
by the reticuloendothelial system, in particular, the splenic macrophages.

79
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:05,960
Recall that the splenic macrophages, when they eat the red blood cells,

80
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:08,200
they break down the hemoglobin into hemoglobin,

81
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:11,100
and then the heme is broken down into iron and protoporphyrin.

82
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:15,560
The iron is recycled, but the protoporphyrin is converted to unconjugated bilirubin.

83
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:19,820
Once the liver conjugates it, it will throw all that extra bilirubin into the bile,

84
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:22,420
and now you've got a large amount of bilirubin in the bile,

85
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:25,480
increasing the risk for the formation of a bilirubin gallstone.

86
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:29,300
Biliary tract infection can also increase the risk for bilirubin gallstone.

87
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,720
Bacteria, when they're present within the biliary tract, can deconjugate the bilirubin,

88
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:38,000
and deconjugated bilirubin increases the risk for precipitation of bilirubin,

89
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:40,500
hence leading to a risk of bilirubin gallstones.

90
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:45,140
Gallstones are usually asymptomatic, and most patients walking around with gallstones don't have symptoms.

91
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:49,260
However, there are five key complications that can result from gallstones,

92
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:52,280
and I want to talk about each of these over the next few slides.

93
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:56,560
Biliary colic is when the patients present with waxing and waning right upper quadrant pain,

94
00:05:56,720 --> 00:06:01,320
and this is due to the gallbladder contracting against the stone that's lodged in the cystic duct.

95
00:06:01,540 --> 00:06:02,360
Here's the idea.

96
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:06,140
Here's the gallbladder here, and this would be the cystic duct here.

97
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:10,640
Now, if a stone were to get lodged in the cystic duct, the gallbladder would then press against it,

98
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:13,060
and as the gallbladder tried to squeeze against it,

99
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:17,280
that would result in a waxing and waning right upper quadrant pain called biliary colic.

100
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:20,620
And so that's one complication that can arise when patients have gallstones.

101
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:25,500
Now, the symptoms would be relieved if this stone were to pass through the entire system and go into the duodenum.

102
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:28,980
However, you could get obstruction here in the common bile duct,

103
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:32,740
and that would increase the risk for acute pancreatitis or obstructive jaundice.

104
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:36,880
And you're going to hear much more about obstructive jaundice when we reach the liver section.

105
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:41,240
Acute cholecystitis is when you get acute inflammation of the gallbladder wall.

106
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:45,260
The idea here is that you've got a stone impacted or stuck within the cystic duct.

107
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:49,020
Because this is stuck, pressure is going to build up here in the gallbladder wall.

108
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:52,580
And as pressure builds up, that's going to squeeze the blood vessels of the wall,

109
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,000
which is going to decrease the blood supply to the gallbladder.

110
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:57,340
Eventually, this gallbladder will become ischemic.

111
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,840
There will be bacterial overgrowth, and then the patients will get inflammation.

112
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:03,320
And that is called acute cholecystitis.

113
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:08,620
Acute cholecystitis will present with right upper quadrant pain that radiates to the right scapula.

114
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:10,940
That's high yield, it will radiate to the right scapula.

115
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:14,540
Of course, they'll also have fever and an elevated white blood cell count,

116
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:15,760
and nausea and vomiting.

117
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:18,520
They can also have an increased serum alkaline phosphatase,

118
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:22,440
and that's because the epithelium that lines the gallbladder and the biliary tract

119
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,940
contains alkaline phosphatase, and so as those cells are damaged and die,

120
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:28,540
alkaline phosphatase will be released within the blood.

121
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:31,420
And finally, there's a risk of rupture if left untreated.

122
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,440
As the wall becomes further damaged, it can obviously rupture.

123
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:38,520
Chronic cholecystitis is when there's chronic inflammation of the gallbladder,

124
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:42,660
due to chemical irritation, and this is from long-standing cholelithiasis.

125
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:47,260
One of the hallmarks of chronic cholecystitis is formation of Rokitansky-Ashoff sinuses.

126
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:51,720
Here's a picture of such a sinus, and notice that here is the gallbladder mucosa up here.

127
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,040
Now, normally, you shouldn't have the gallbladder mucosa diving down

128
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:57,340
into the smooth muscle of the gallbladder wall.

129
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:00,180
However, you can clearly see that there's these little outpouchings

130
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:03,800
of gallbladder mucosa diving down in between the smooth muscle,

131
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,180
and that's called a Rokitansky-Ashoff sinus,

132
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:08,960
and that's particularly high yield for examination purposes.

133
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:12,940
Now, these patients will present with vague right upper quadrant pain,

134
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:17,220
especially after eating, and porcelain gallbladder is a late complication.

135
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:20,000
The more there's chemical damage to the wall of the gallbladder,

136
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:23,000
the more you increase the risk for dystrophic calcification,

137
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:25,880
which can then result in something called a porcelain gallbladder.

138
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:27,840
Here's a picture of a porcelain gallbladder.

139
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:30,940
You can clearly see that the wall of the gallbladder has become calcified.

140
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:33,140
Again, that's called porcelain gallbladder.

141
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,660
The treatment, of course, would be cholecystectomy,

142
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:37,100
which is removal of the gallbladder.

143
00:08:37,340 --> 00:08:40,100
You especially want to do this if porcelain gallbladder is present

144
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:42,400
because porcelain gallbladder is a risk factor

145
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:44,200
for the formation of gallbladder cancer.

146
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,340
Ascending cholangitis is another complication that can arise with bile stones,

147
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:51,360
and this is when you get bacterial infection of the bile ducts.

148
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,100
Now, the word ascending tells you a lot

149
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:55,500
because it's usually due to ascending infection

150
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:57,620
with enteric gram-negative bacteria.

151
00:08:57,620 --> 00:09:01,620
So you get bacteria within the bowel that sort of walk their way up the ductal system.

152
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:05,520
Now, this is going to present a sepsis, jaundice, and abdominal pain,

153
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:08,800
and there's an increased incidence with choledochal lithiasis.

154
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:12,400
Again, the idea here is that you've got enteric gram-negative bacteria

155
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:14,160
that walk their way up the ductal system,

156
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:17,620
and as they do so, that's going to result in an ascending cholangitis.

157
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:21,120
Now, remember that the bile from the gallbladder is constantly flowing,

158
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:23,840
and as it flows, it normally washes away the bacteria

159
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:25,960
so that they can't rise up into this duct.

160
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:29,300
However, if you had, for example, choledochal lithiasis,

161
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:30,760
which would be a stone within the duct,

162
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:32,420
then, of course, there would be less flow,

163
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:34,280
and so the bacteria could work their way up here

164
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,700
and eventually cause an ascending cholangitis.

165
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:38,820
And that's why I stated here in your text

166
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:42,300
that there is an increased incidence with choledochal lithiasis.

167
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:46,040
Gallstone ileus is when a gallstone enters and obstructs the small bowel.

168
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:49,740
Now, the way by which this occurs is that a patient has cholecystitis

169
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:52,940
and then forms a fistula between the gallbladder and the small bowel.

170
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:56,440
Basically, the idea here is that you've got inflammation of the gallbladder wall.

171
00:09:56,780 --> 00:09:58,540
This causes rupture of the gallbladder wall,

172
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:02,460
and a fistula is formed between the gallbladder wall and the duodenum.

173
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:05,520
Of course, large stones can then enter into the small bowel,

174
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:07,520
which could potentially obstruct the bowel,

175
00:10:07,820 --> 00:10:09,700
and that would be called a gallstone ileus.

176
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:12,280
Gallbladder carcinoma is an adenocarcinoma,

177
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:15,360
and it arises from the glandular epithelium

178
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:17,180
that normally lines the gallbladder wall.

179
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:19,580
Now, one of the major risk factors are gallstones,

180
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:22,160
especially when a patient has a porcelain gallbladder.

181
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:26,960
Gallbladder carcinoma classically presents as cholecystitis in an elderly woman.

182
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:31,380
Now, remember that cholecystitis is basically a disease of 40-year-olds and 50-year-olds.

183
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:34,640
However, if you have a very old woman that presents with, for example,

184
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:37,680
new-onset cholecystitis, especially on a board exam,

185
00:10:37,860 --> 00:10:41,380
you really want to think about gallbladder carcinoma as an important cause,

186
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:43,840
and this has a particularly poor prognosis.

187
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:46,180
Here's a picture of the gallbladder cut open,

188
00:10:46,180 --> 00:10:48,760
and here you can see that there's a tumor growing on the wall,

189
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:51,440
and this tumor represents gallbladder carcinoma.

190
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:54,000
And that concludes this particular section.

